Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> If you are going to do this as a new effort in
> today's software writing "best practice" model, you
> really, really, really want to write this portable
> bones file in what has become the software industry
> standard for portable files across pretty much all
> applications types: XML.

I think XML is more about exchanging data between programs, not
exchanging data between different instances of the same program. After
all, if you have control over the source code of both the file writer
and file reader, why does it need to be in a portable format? Just
write the data unambiguously (sans endianness issues and the rest) and
make the reading code slightly cleverer than it is.

True, XML bones files would be nice for 3rd-party apps like NAO and
hearse to interpret, but it'll be a cold day in Gehennom before NH
depends on an XML library.

